why-structs.content:
### Unifying Scattered Variables

Until now, when tracking Scout Drone Sigma, your code had to juggle separate disconnected variables:
```cpp
int drone_id = 1;
int battery = 95;
double altitude = 42.5;
bool is_active = true;
```
What happens when we add Cartographer Delta and Cargo Drone Omega? You would have to declare 12 independent variables! Passing a drone to a function would require 4 separate arguments every single time.

In C++, real-world entities are modeled by creating **composite user-defined types** using the **`struct`** keyword.

A `struct` bundles heterogeneous variables (integers, doubles, booleans) into a single cohesive data unit in memory:
```cpp
struct Drone {
    int id;
    int battery;
    double altitude;
    bool is_active;
};
```
Now, a drone is treated as a first-class object: instantiated in one line, passed to functions cleanly, and organized into structured systems.

